Overview:
My indy project is a side-scrolling running game inspired by "Jetpack Joyride". App launches the application
and sets the stage. The PaneOrganizer class is the highest graphical class and initializes the user interface elements
as well as the game. The Game class is the highest logical class and manages the main gameplay timeline, including
score tracking, collision detection, and spawning obstacles and power-ups/transformations by using a world generator
algorithm. The TransformationType enum stores all the types of vehicles, and is used by the queuedTransformation class
which is a wrapper for the transformation vehicle type (instead of directly transforming when collecting powerup).
The WorldGenerator class is the game’s main algorithm responsible for procedural generation of obstacles (like Zapper,
Rocket, Laser) according to changing game speed and difficulty parameters, and other extensive condition checking.
Vehicles are represented by classes that extend the abstract Vehicle class (e.g., Avatar, Stomper, Motorcycle, Dragon,
GravitySuit) and each have unique movement mechanics. Different powerups (vehicle, zoom, shield, and slowmo) are able
to be collected and provide special abilities to the vehicles.

Design Choices:
Polymorphism (Inheritance) for Vehicles and Obstacles:
I chose to use inheritance for vehicles, powerups, and obstacles.  All extended abstract classes with multiple
subclasses representing different vehicle types. This allows each to share traits while implementing distinct behaviors
for movement and input handling. The method calls can then be more generic. For vehicles, all shared a velocity,
thrustActive, a vehicleGroup visual variable, but had unique movement, graphical removal, and keypress mechanics. For
obstacles, all shared a vehicle, pane, and gamespeed reference, an active flag, but had unique movement and collision
detection methods. For powerups, all were represented by an icon modeled by a javafx.scene.Group, moved and collided
the same way, but had different specific visuals and triggered different special abilities.

Enum for Transformation, Vehicle, and Obstacle Types:
The TransformationType includes all possible vehicle transformations. Instead of using strings or constants, the
transformation enum provides direct mapping to methods that create the appropriate vehicle instances corresponding to
each type. Vehicle type and obstacle type enums list out all possible types and getter methods for the types allow
for proper object type checking for conditional obstacle generation/mechanics in game and algorithm.

Data Structures (Queues and Arrays):
A Queue<QueuedTransformation> is a wrapper of the transformation to store upcoming vehicle transformations types, and
is fed into the queue so when collision is detected, the game knows the transformation to perform. Using a FIFO queue,
transformations occur in the order they are collected. Both shields and vehicle transformations are able to be queued
infinitely. For obstacles and power-ups, ArrayList allows looping/iterating when checking collisions and removing
expired obstacles and power ups.

Semi-Random World Generation:
The WorldGenerator uses a combination of random values and condition checking (like MAX_SIMULTANEOUS_OBSTACLES and
MAX_Y_AXIS_COVERAGE) to produce a balanced, semi-random distribution of obstacles, and model the original game. This
ensures playability and prevents scenarios where the character has no playable space.

Locking and Unlocking Game Speed:
During power-ups or transformations, the WorldGenerator can lock or unlock the game speed. This allows players to adapt
to transformations, collisions, and prevent sudden difficulty changes. This design is more flexible than hard-coding
speed changes into the main game.

Hours Spent: 60

